{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"reasoning","text":"Very informative! Key findings:\n\n1. Insert size distribution: median 467, MAD 20, but the max is 500?! p99=500, max=500. The max insert for FR proper pairs is exactly 500 — this is because bowtie2's -X default of 500 caps the concordant alignment window! Pairs whose true insert exceeds 500 can't be concordant, and got realigned as \"discordant\" — but the majority of pairs are FR orientation (328,008 pairs are FR). Of those, only 169,570 are proper (≤500bp). So the remaining ~158k FR pairs have an insert >500, exceeding the -X limit → got counted as discordant.\n\nTherefore the library's fragment length distribution is wider than 500. Need to rerun alignment with a larger -X (e.g., -X 20000) so that pairs with long inserts align concordantly, or else just analyze as-is: FR pairs with pos distance >500 are already aligned (both mates), and their coordinates can be computed directly. Since both mates are aligned, we can compute the outer span ourselves regardless of bowtie2's proper-pair classification. The issue is whether bowtie2 picked the correct alignment for those pairs when it treated them as discordant. For FR pairs with large spans, bowtie2's discordant search… hmm, actually bowtie2 only tries alternative orientations in its discordant search; for an FR pair with mates 2kb apart, does it report that as a \"discordant alignment\"? Yes — and the alignment itself is probably correct (each mate's best position).\n\nBut there's a risk: pairs that span a deletion breakpoint have mates flanking the deletion, separated by (fragment length + deletion size), e.g., 2 Mb apart. Bowtie2 might align those mates correctly (each to its own unique position) — as long as the mates map uniquely, we're fine.\n\n2. Depth profile: coverage is ~270k bp per 100kb bin → depth ≈ 2.7x outside; zero bins correspond to N-gap regions (0–10 Mb is short arm/centromeric Ns; also zero bins at 13–15 Mb and 20–21 Mb — hmm interesting: bins 13-14 Mb are 0, 15-20 are ~270k, bins 20-21 are 0, 21-51 are ~270k).\n\nWait — actually looking more carefully: coverage is zero in bins 13–14 Mb and 20–21 Mb. Are these N-gaps in the chr22 reference? GRCh38 chr22 has known gaps: around the centromere, ~10.5-12.4 Mb? And there are also gaps in the 13-15 Mb region and the 20 Mb region (segmental duplication/centromere-proximal gaps). Let's verify the N-runs of the reference directly.\n\nCrucially, I don't yet see a clear heterozygous deletion dip (a region with ~half the depth). All non-gap bins are ~270k ± 10%. Hmm. So where is the deletion? If the deletion were present in the sample relative to GRCh38, depth in that region should drop by half (heterozygous) or to zero (homozygous). I don't see that in the 100kb bins!\n\nHowever… if the deletion is very large (multi-Mb), and the depth within the deletion is still ~270k because… no wait, that can't be. Let me think again.\n\nWait — could the deletion be homozygous, and the depth within the deletion be ~0? The zero bins at 13-14 Mb and 20-21 Mb — if these aren't N-gaps in the reference but truly have no reads, that would be a homozygous deletion! But the GRCh38 chr22 reference actually does have N-gaps there. Need to verify.\n\nOr: the deletion is present in the sample genome used for simulation (i.e., reads are generated from a genome with a deletion relative to GRCh38). Then:\n- Reads from the deleted haplotype: pairs spanning the breakpoint map with huge inserts.\n- Depth: heterozygous deletion → 50% depth in the region; homozygous → ~0 depth.\n\nI don't see a 50% dip anywhere. Hmm, so maybe the deletion is on the reference side: the sample is the reference (no deletion)… no, that can't be.\n\nOr: the reads are simulated from a genome where a segment of chr22 is deleted, and the simulation only generates reads from the deleted genome (haploid simulation). Then, depth in the deleted region = 0 (no reads originate from there). The zero bins at 13-15 Mb and 20-21 Mb become candidates, if they aren't reference N-gaps.\n\nWait, but bins 13.0–14.9 Mb: two consecutive 100kb bins are 0. And 20–21 Mb: two bins are 0. Let's check the N-structure of the reference.\n\nAlso interesting: bin 10-11 Mb (second entry: 256k) — the first half of 10-11 Mb (10.0-10.5) is probably an N-gap (centromeric), so the effective mappable length is smaller, but the coverage is nearly full 256k? Hmm, if 10.0-10.5 is Ns, then the mappable portion is 10.5-11.0 Mb = 50kb, and cov=256k bp corresponds to depth ~5x over the mappable portion — 2x that of the flanks. Hmm, this could be the boundary effect of a deletion!\n\nLet me think again about what a deletion looks like in depth: a deletion of region [A,B] in the sample relative to the reference means no reads from [A,B] → depth ~0 (haploid/homozygous) or ~half (heterozygous) in [A,B].\n\nWhat I see: all bins between 15-51 Mb are uniformly ~270k (±5%). The zero bins are in the known N-gap regions (13-15, 20-21). So there is no depth dip suggesting a deletion in the sample relative to the reference…\n\nHowever, if the situation is reversed: the reference chr22 is missing a segment that is present in the sample? No — the task is to \"identify a large deletion in the provided data\" — a deletion in the sample relative to hg38.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I need to look more carefully at bin 10-11 Mb and the boundary regions. Bin 10-10.5: 0, bin 10.5-11: 256k? The output shows for bin index 100 (10.0-10.1 Mb) \"10-10 Mb cov=0\", then at i=105 \"10-10 Mb cov=256076\"? The printed label is truncated ((i+1)*BIN//1000000 gives 10 for 10.x and 11 for 10.9-11.0… whatever). The pattern: zero up to about 10.5 Mb, then 256k at 10.5-11, 127k at 11-11.5, 123k at 11.5-12, 188k at 12-12.5, 267k at 12.5-13, zero at 13-15, then normal.\n\nHmm, the 11-12.5 Mb region shows ~half coverage (127k, 123k ≈ half of 270k)! And 12-12.5 is intermediate (188k). This looks like a heterozygous deletion spanning roughly ~11.0-12.0 Mb, with fuzzy boundaries due to the N-gap structure.\n\nAnd the zero bins at 13-15 and 20-21: need to check whether they're reference Ns. GRCh38 chr22: is there a gap at 13.7-14.4 Mb? Actually, GRCh38 chr22 has several gaps: the centromere is roughly 12.9-14.4? Hmm. Let me check the N-runs in the reference fasta directly.\n\nAnd the bin 20-21 Mb = 0: GRCh38 chr22 has a gap around 20-21 Mb? There is indeed a gap: chr22:20,000,000? Not sure. Let's compute.\n\nAlso, a heterozygous deletion at ~11-12 Mb… but wait, there's a subtlety: the region 10.5-13 Mb on chr22 contains the chr22-specific segmental duplication… whatever. Let's get the exact N-gaps of the reference, and also do a finer depth profile (10kb bins) in the region 10-16 Mb.\n\nAlso the FR insert analysis: I was filtering only \"proper\" pairs (flag 0x2). I need all FR pairs on chr22 with mapq filtering, compute the outer span, and look for a cluster of huge spans (>2kb). That cluster's left-mate-end/right-mate-start positions will pinpoint the breakpoints.\n\nLet's also check where the 134k \"discordant\" pairs are: their mate-span distribution. If the deletion is heterozygous at ~11-12 Mb, spanning pairs should cluster around the breakpoints. But at 2.7x depth, the number of spanning pairs is small (at most ~10-30 pairs). Hmm, so 134k discordant pairs is mostly due to the insert distribution exceeding 500? Let's check the span distribution of FR non-proper pairs.\n\nWait, actually the insert median is 467 and the max is capped at 500. p95=497 means the fragment distribution is tight around ~460-470 with small variance… so pairs with span >500 should be rare (<1%) — yet 162k pairs are non-concordant. Contradiction → many pairs have mates mapping far apart (multi-kb to Mb spans). Those are the \"discordant\" ones. But that's too many (40%) to be explained by a single deletion breakpoint. Something else is going on: the simulation might have generated reads from a genome with a large inversion? Or… hmm.\n\n