eference there. Interesting!! Could this be a homozygous deletion? A 200kb homozygous deletion? Hmm, but then what about the half-depth bins at 11–12 Mb...\n\nHmm, wait, actually. Let me reconsider: could the zero coverage at 20–20.2 Mb be because reads there are multi-mappers (MAPQ 0) and I filtered with mapq<1? chr22 20–21 Mb... hmm, actually there's something: GRCh38 chr22 around 20 Mb — is there an LCR/segmental duplication? Hmm.\n\nWait, actually — I recall that the GRCh38 chr22 assembly has gaps: per the UCSC gap track, chr22 has gaps:\n- 10,510,001-10,532,410? No...\n\nHmm, I don't trust memory. Let me look at the fasta directly: the 19.9–20.3 Mb region — what's the sequence like? If it's real sequence (no Ns) and zero uniquely-mapped coverage, that's a candidate deletion region. But hmm, 2x shallow coverage: the probability of zero reads covering a 200kb region by chance at 2.7x depth is ~e^{-540} — impossible. So a 200kb zero-coverage region = definitely a real signal: either reference gap (no), mappability issue (unlikely to be completely zero), or homozygous deletion in the sample.\n\nHmm wait, but wait: could the simulation have deleted 20–20.2 Mb from the sample genome? And the half-depth bins at 11–12 Mb might be another deletion (heterozygous)? Let me do this carefully. Let me compute the normalized depth (per mappable bp) for all bins, and also do fine-grained (10kb or 5kb) depth in the candidate regions.\n\nAlso the FR span outliers: 80 pairs with span >10kb, 30 pairs with span >1 Mb, max span 17.4 Mb (!). The 30 pairs with >1 Mb and 29 pairs with 0.2–1 Mb: these are breakpoint-spanning candidates. Let me get their exact coordinates — cluster them.\n\nWait, but there's a subtle point: many pairs with span >500 were aligned by bowtie2 as \"discordant\" — and bowtie2's discordant search might place the mate at a wrong alternative position. But if each mate maps uniquely (high MAPQ), the positions are reliable. Let me require MAPQ ≥ 20 (or ≥10) for bot