{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"reasoning","text":"With this, the situation is becoming fairly clear.\n\n**Depth profile:** There's an approximately 1 Mb region with near-zero coverage at **20.0–21.0 Mb** (bins 20.0–20.9: coverage 0, 887, 5032, 24462, 150, 0, 5841, 6134, 0, 7). Everything else is at the baseline of about 2.7x (normalized to 2.6–2.9). So this is a **homozygous deletion** (the sample has no reads from this region). The small amounts of residual coverage (887, 5032, 24462, 150, 5841, 6134) are probably reads from the flanks extending into the bin boundaries, or multi-mappers (I filtered mapq<1, but reads with mapq≥1 that partially overlap the boundary, or reads whose mates are... hmm, actually, reads that start inside the deleted region can't exist; the residuals are probably reads overlapping the bin boundary (read end inside the bin) or multi-mappers from paralogous regions). The largest residual is in bin 20.3–20.4 (24462 bp ≈ 0.24x) — hmm, this might be a segmental duplication paralog elsewhere on chr22 where reads map with MAPQ≥1... or it might indicate that the deletion boundary is within 20.3–20.4.\n\nWait, actually, if the deletion is [A,B], reads from the sample don't cover [A,B]. But reads starting just before A extend rightward up to 150bp into the deleted region; also, FR fragments starting before A span the breakpoint, with the right mate starting after B. Residual coverage inside [A,B] only from left mates starting before A and extending inward ≤150 bp. So near-zero coverage is expected, with a slight rise-up exactly at A (at most ~150 bp). The 24462 bp inside the 20.3–20.4 bin suggests either that A is within 20.3–20.4 (reads piling up at the left edge of the deletion contribute up to ~150bp × depth each...). Hmm, 24462 bp of coverage in one 100kb bin — that's equivalent to 0.24x. If the deletion starts around position ~20.35 Mb, then reads ending at... no wait, reads can't extend into the deleted region more than the read length from the left flank: maximum coverage inside the deletion from flank leakage = depth × 150 bp distributed as a linear decay over 150 bp, totaling depth×150 ≈ 400 bp·x... that's tiny (2.7x × 150bp = 405 bp of coverage). So 24462 bp can't be flank leakage. It must be reads mapping to a paralogous region (multi-copy, mapq≥1), or the deletion isn't fully homozygous... or the reads are from a duplicated segment.\n\nHmm, wait, there's another possibility: is the 20–21 Mb region a known segmental duplication on chr22? There are LCRs on chr22... but depth outside the 20–21 Mb region is normal everywhere, so paralogous reads should distribute to the paralogous copies elsewhere, not pile up inside 20.3–20.4.\n\nLet me think about the spanning pairs instead — these are decisive:\n\n**Long-span FR pair cluster:**\n1. left_start ~10.65–10.69 Mb, right_start ~16.87–16.90 Mb, span ≈ 6.22 Mb, consistent at about 6,219,362–6,219,415. Implied breakpoints: left_end ≈ 10.65–10.69 (left mate end), right_start ≈ 16.87–16.90. Hmm, wait — for a deletion [A,B], a spanning pair has the left mate ending just before A and the right mate starting just after B. Here left_end ≈ 10.65–10.69 Mb, right_start ≈ 16.87–16.90 Mb. That would imply a deletion of about 6.2 Mb from about 10.67 to about 16.88?? But depth is normal at 15.2–16 Mb (2.72, 2.68, 2.88, 2.65...)! A deletion can't have normal depth in its interior. So these pairs are NOT spanning a deletion...\n\nWait, wait. Hmm. What produces FR pairs with left mates at about 10.66 and right mates at about 16.88? If the sample genome had an inversion, or... hmm. Or — wait. What if these reads come from a **chimeric/deleted haplotype** and the mapping positions are... let me think about the ~15.2→16.0 Mb pairs: left 15.18–15.26, right 16.00–16.13, span ~825–875 kb. And the ~18.8→21.2 Mb pairs: left 18.79–18.82, right 21.23–21.25, span ≈ 2.435 Mb, very consistent (2,434,866–2,435,005). And ~22.6→24.6: left 22.63, right 24.64, span ~2.016 Mb (two pairs) plus others. And ~23.3→24.3/24.6. And ~25.2–25.3→25.5–25.6: many pairs, spans 150–304 kb. And ~19.9997→21.0001: two pairs, span ~1.0005 Mb!!\n\nInteresting!! The pair left_start=19,999,646 → right_start=21,000,059, span 1,000,563 bp. This is exactly the zero-depth region [20.0–21.0 Mb]! Left mate ends at 19,999,795 (just before 20.0 Mb), right mate starts at 21,000,059 (just after 21.0 Mb). Span ≈ fragment + deletion. If the fragment is ~467, then the deletion is ~999.6 kb... wait: span = right_end - left_start + 1 = 1,000,563. Right mate length 150, left 150. Outer span = left_read + insert_between_reads + right_read. Deletion size D ≈ span - expected_fragment_length... hmm, let me define: on the sample genome, the fragment covers [x, x+F] with F≈467. The left read covers [x, x+150]. The right read covers [x+F-150, x+F] on the sample. On the reference, the sample's [A,B] is deleted: reference coordinates ≥ B shift left by D=B-A+1... no wait, reference = GRCh38, sample has the deletion. Sample position p < A maps to reference p. Sample position p > B maps to reference p - D, where D = B - A + 1. Left read ends at sample x+150 < A (must be upstream of deletion), right read starts at sample x+F-150 > B. Right read's reference start = (x+F-150) - D + ... hmm: reference_pos = sample_pos - D for positions > B. Right_start_ref = x + F - 150 - D... no wait:\n\nSample coordinate s; reference coordinate r: r = s for s < A; r = s - D for s > B (D = B - A + 1, the deleted interval length).\n\nLeft read: sample [x, x+149], all < A. left_start_ref = x, left_end_ref = x+149.\nRight read: sample [x+F-150, x+F-1], all > B. right_start_ref = x+F-150-D, right_end_ref = x+F-1-D.\nSpan_ref = right_end_ref - left_start_ref + 1 = F - D.\n\nWait, that gives span = F - D?? No wait: span_ref = (x+F-1-D) - x + 1 = F - D. Hmm, so it's less than the fragment length? That can't be right... oh wait, I got it backwards. The sample has less DNA (deletion), so a fragment spanning the deletion covers more reference coordinates. Let me redo: sample coordinates are compressed relative to reference. Sample position s > B corresponds to reference position s + D (because the reference has D extra bases [A,B] that the sample lacks). Let me re-derive: reference sequence = sample sequence with [A,B] inserted... no wait — the sample has the deletion relative to the reference. Reference = GRCh38. Sample = GRCh38 minus [A,B].\n\nSample coordinate s: for s < A, reference r = s. For sample positions past the deletion: sample base just after the deleted segment corresponds to reference B+1. So r = s + D, where D = B - A + 1.\n\nRight read sample [x+F-150, x+F-1] → reference [x+F-150+D, x+F-1+D].\nSpan_ref = (x+F-1+D) - x + 1 = F + D. ✓ So span = fragment + deletion. Good, I had it right originally.\n\nFor the pair: left_start=19,999,646, left_end=19,999,795 (150 bp), right_start=21,000,059, right_end=21,000,208 (150 bp), span=1,000,563 = F + D, F≈467 → D ≈ 999,595... but hmm wait: also left_end should be ≈ A - something and right_start ≈ B + something. Specifically, for the fragment to span the deletion: x+149 < A and x+F-150+D > B... left mate's reference end = x+149, which can be anywhere upstream of A (within fragment length). If F≈467: x+149 ≥ A - (F-150) = A - 317. So left_end ∈ [A-317, A-1]. Similarly right_start ∈ [B+1, B+317].\n\nThe two pairs at 20/21: left_end = 19,999,795 and 19,999,821 → A ∈ [19,999,796, 20,000,112]... right_start = 21,000,059 and 21,000,014 → B ∈ [20,999,697, 21,000,058]. Combining with the depth edge, the deletion is ≈ [~20,000,000, ~21,000,000], size ≈ 1 Mb. D = span - F: spans are 1,000,563 and 1,000,492; F ≈ 467±? → D ≈ 1,000,100 and 1,000,025. Consistent with D ≈ 1,000,000 ± few hundred. Also the \"clean\" round numbers (20.0 Mb, 21.0 Mb) suggest the simulation placed the deletion exactly at chr22:20,000,000–21,000,000 (a tidy 1 Mb deletion).\n\nOK — so what about all the other long-span clusters (10.66→16.88, 15.2→16.0, 18.8→21.2, 22.6→24.6, 23.3→24.3, 25.2→25.5, 27.7→34.8, 11.26→28.67)? Those can't all be deletions — the depth is uniformly normal outside 20–21 Mb. What produces recurrent FR pairs with a constant span between two specific loci? **Tandem-duplication-type artifacts? No — these look like reads from paralogous/segmental-duplication regions being mismapped.** chr22 is famous for its segmental duplications (e.g., the ~1.4 Mb region 16–17 Mb duplicated elsewhere? The chr22q11.2 region, LCR22s...). If two paralogous copies exist within the reference, reads from one copy can be mapped split across both copies, producing apparent FR pairs with large spans. Example: the left mate maps to copy 1 (10.66 Mb), the right mate maps to copy 2 (16.88 Mb) — if copy 1 and copy 2 are ~6.22 Mb apart and the reads are 100% identical within the shared segment, then a fragment sequenced from either copy can map with left→copy 1, right→copy 2.\n\nBut wait — MAPQ filter ≥10: multi-mapping reads should have MAPQ 0 in bowtie2. bowtie2 assigns MAPQ based on uniqueness of the best alignment, but... hmm, for paired-end, bowtie2's MAPQ considers the uniqueness of the concordant alignment. These pairs are discordant (span >500), so bowtie2 found them via discordant search; the MAPQ may reflect uniqueness of each mate individually. If mates map uniquely within their paralog context (each mate has a unique best position due to paralog-distinguishing variants), then the pairs are... hmm, then they wouldn't map to the wrong copy.\n\nAlternatively: maybe these are **real duplicated segments in the sample genome** (the simulation might have included them?). But the task says \"identify the big deletion\" — singular. Other SV signals would be distractors from segmental duplications in the reference.\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider: are these clusters in known chr22 segmental duplication blocks?\n- chr22 ~10.5–12.9 Mb pericentromeric region: contains large segmental duplications? Hmm.\n- The 16–17 Mb region: chr22q11.2 LCR22 blocks (the typical 3 Mb 22q11.2 deletion region is flanked by LCR22-A and -D, at ~14.4–17.7 Mb?). Hmm, LCR22s mediate the typical 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge).\n- The ~22.6–24.6 Mb region: hmm.\n\nLet me check which of these clusters are consistent with segmental duplications: pairs with left mates in [X1,X2] and right mates in [Y1,Y2], with span ≈ Y-X constant ≈ (distance between paralogous copies). For a pair of paralogous copies at positions P1 and P2 in the reference (P2-P1 = Δ), reads from a fragment within copy k: left mate can map to P1 and right mate to P2 if the cross-mapping is consistent: left_read_ref = P1 + offset, right_read_ref = P2 + offset + (F-150)... so span = P2 - P1 + F. The observed spans cluster at constant values: 6,219,362–6,219,415 (Δ≈6.2189 Mb), 2,434,866–2,435,005 (Δ≈2.4344 Mb), 2,015–2,016 kb, 825–875 kb, 225–304 kb, etc. These constant-Δ clusters = paralogous copies at fixed distances. This is the classic segmental-duplication signature, not real SVs.\n\nAnd importantly, these paralog-mediated pairs have both MAPQ ≥10 because... hmm, bowtie2 MAPQ for discordant pairs — each mate aligns uniquely? If copies are nearly identical, mates shouldn't be unique. Unless bowtie2's discordant search rescues them with moderate MAPQ. Oh well — they're a known distractor.\n\nBut hmm, wait: could the ~19.9997→21.000059 cluster (span ~1.0005 Mb) also be a segmental duplication pair?? If there were paralogs at 20.0 and 21.0 Mb... but the depth profile shows zero coverage across 20.0–21.0 Mb (except a small residual), which is inconsistent with a paralog pair (paralogs should show normal depth in both copies). The zero depth decisively indicates a deletion. Also, there are only two pairs in the 20/21 cluster, consistent with low coverage.\n\nHmm, but wait — let me reconsider the residual coverage in 20.3–20.4 (24462 bp ≈ 0.24x) and smaller residuals elsewhere (0.01–0.06x). If the deletion is truly [20.0M, 21.0M], then residual coverage inside could come from reads originating in the paralogous copies (e.g., reads from a segmental duplication on another chromosome or elsewhere on chr22 mapping here with MAPQ≥1 by chance). The fact that the 20.3–20.4 bin has the most suggests a paralog with ~0.24x... hmm, or maybe the deletion boundary isn't exactly at 20.0/21.0: if the deletion is [20,024,462, 21,024,462] or something, the edge bins would shift. The spanning pair left_ends are 19,999,795 and 19,999,821 → both < 20.0M, and right_starts are 21,000,014 and 21,000,059 → both just above 21.0M. For left_end ∈ [A-317, A-1] we need A ≥ left_end+1 and A ≤ left_end+318. So A ∈ [19,999,796+1, 19,999,821+318] = [19,999,797, 20,000,139]. B: right_start ∈ [B+1, B+318] → B ∈ [right_start-318, right_start-1] = [20,999,696, 21,000,058]. Combined with depth edges (zero from ~20.0 to ~21.0), the deletion is ≈ chr22:19,999,800–21,000,050 or so, i.e. ~1.0 Mb, consistent with the simulation having chosen round coordinates 20,000,000–21,000,000.\n\nHmm wait, but let me double-check the edges more precisely with the coverage. Depth at 19.9–20.0 bin is full (2.65x), 21.0–21.1 is full (2.74x). Residuals: 20.1–20.2: 887 bp; 20.2–20.3: 5032; 20.3–20.4: 24462; 20.4–20.5: 150; 20.5–20.6: 0; 20.6–20.7: 5841; 20.7–20.8: 6134; 20.8–20.9: 0; 20.9–21.0: 7. These residuals total ~42 kb — probably multi-mappers/paralogous reads with MAPQ≥1, plus a few edge reads. To refine the breakpoints, let me look at 5-kb resolution depth around 19.95–20.10 and 20.90–21.05 Mb. And examine the actual residual reads inside 20.0–21.0: what MAPQ do they have, what are their mates doing?\n\nAlso, the same-strand pairs cluster in bins 118–123 (11.8–12.3 Mb: 123 pairs) — that's the pericentromeric segmental-duplication region; also RF pairs scatter at 106, 152–153, 185 — paralog regions. Not a deletion signal.\n\nSo the answer: **homozygous deletion of ~1 Mb at chr22:20,000,000–21,000,000 (GRCh38)**.\n\nNow, before finalizing, let me double-check the possibility of a heterozygous deletion elsewhere: the normalized depth plot showed all bins (with mappability ≥50kb) at 2.35–2.97 except 20.0–21.0. The 11–13 Mb bins were slightly low (2.35–2.44) but that's noise from small mappability (50–52 kb mappable → high variance). No heterozygous (half-depth) region. So a single homozygous deletion of ~1 Mb.\n\nHmm wait, one more check — bins with mappability <50kb weren't output. That's fine.\n\nAlso, should I check whether any reads map outside chr22? No, the reference is chr22-only, so all mapped reads are on chr22. QC note: reads from other chromosomes (if the sample had them) would either fail to map or mismap. The overall alignment rate was 100%, suggesting pure chr22 data.\n\nOK — but hmm, let me reconsider the 15.2→16.0 Mb cluster and others once more: could any of them represent real deletions? No depth dips anywhere else, so no. They must be paralog-mediated. But actually, wait: could they be spanning pairs of a heterozygous deletion where the depth drop is masked? A heterozygous deletion would show 50% depth — I'd see it. There's no such region. OK.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider the 10.66→16.88 pairs once more: left_end ≈ 10.655–10.690, right_start ≈ 16.874–16.902, Δ = span - F ≈ 6.2189 Mb. If the sample had a deletion from ~10.67 to ~16.88, depth would be zero/half across 10.7–16.9 — clearly not observed. So definitely not. Paralog artifacts. (chr22 has a known ~6.2 Mb-spaced segmental duplication: e.g., LCR22 copies... well, plausible.)\n\nAlso the pair left=11,256,818 → right=28,667,860 (span 17.4 Mb): singletons, probably random mismappings. And 27.74→34.84: singletons. And the 22.67/22.71→... pairs: small clusters in the 22–25 Mb region, spans 59–304 kb: segmental duplications (chr22 has many). Fine.\n\nNow, about the two 20/21 spanning pairs — let me double-check their MAPQ and CIGAR (they had MAPQ≥10 due to the filter). Also search for soft-clipped reads near 20.0M and 21.0M. Earlier I found zero reads with soft-clips ≥20 bp — because bowtie2 in --very-sensitive mode does local alignment... hmm, zero soft-clips in the whole BAM is strange; bowtie2's local alignment should produce soft-clips at breakpoints. Reads crossing the breakpoint: a read starting ~50 bp before A continues into the deleted region — on the sample genome, the read continues into sequence past B. On the reference, this read has the first 50 bp mapping at ~A-50..A and the remaining 100 bp mapping at B+... — that's a split read, but bowtie2 can't do split alignment, so such reads either fail to map or map with many mismatches/soft-clips. If the deletion is exact (sample = reference minus [A,B]), then a breakpoint-crossing read = prefix matching [A-k, A) + suffix matching [B, B+150-k). Bowtie2 (local, no splicing) would soft-clip one side, or fail. Zero soft-clips ≥20 bp means... hmm, bowtie2 in end-to-end mode? No, I used the default local mode for --very-sensitive? Bowtie2's default for unaligned reads is local alignment... actually, bowtie2 does local alignment by default (soft-clipping allowed). Zero reads with soft-clip ≥20 bp in 664k reads is surprising, but maybe reads crossing the breakpoint just failed to map (counted as unmapped, --no-unal suppressed them). Recall: \"3 mates aligned 0 times\" — only 3 mates totally unmapped. Hmm, so breakpoint-crossing reads did map to something — with full alignment and mismatches? At the exact breakpoint, a crossing read has 50 bp perfect match upstream of A and 100 bp perfect match starting at B; bowtie2 local would align one segment and soft-clip the rest. With --very-sensitive, it should find it. But soft-clip count = 0 (with threshold ≥20). Let me check all soft-clips (even small) near 20.0M/21.0M... actually, my clip detection was looking at S operations; maybe bowtie2 reported them with hard-clips? No, bowtie2 uses S. Hmm, or maybe breakpoint-crossing reads map to paralogous regions elsewhere (MAPQ 0, filtered for depth but included in clip check — clip check wasn't filtered by MAPQ). Anyway: total reads with clip ≥20 bp was 0. Interesting.\n\nWait — could it be that the simulated reads don't cross the breakpoint? If the simulator generated reads from the deleted genome, fragments crossing the deletion produce reads crossing the breakpoint. At 2.7x with fragment 467, expected number of breakpoint-crossing fragments ≈ 2 × coverage... number of fragments starting within a read length of the breakpoint ≈ N_pairs × (2×150)/50.8M ≈ 332k × 300/50.8M ≈ 2 fragments per breakpoint. So ~2 split reads per breakpoint expected — small but nonzero. Those might map with soft-clips... or not map. Only 3 mates totally unmapped. Hmm, so the crossing reads mapped somewhere — probably with soft-clips... but I counted zero. Let me re-check: maybe my CIGAR S-detection failed due to the parsing loop (parse_cigar_clip) — looks correct. Or maybe crossing reads got aligned discordantly as spanning pairs! No — spanning pairs are pairs where both mates map separately; crossing reads are single reads.\n\nOh well — a small detail. The two spanning pairs + depth give sufficient evidence. But to strengthen the breakpoint estimate and \"distinguish evidence from precision\", let me refine:\n1. 5 kb (or 1 kb) resolution depth around 19.9–20.15 and 20.85–21.1 Mb.\n2. Examine all reads (any MAPQ) overlapping 19.999–20.001 and 20.999–21.001, check for soft-clips (any size) and mate positions.\n3. Confirm the two spanning pairs' MAPQ/CIGAR.\n\nAfter that, compute the final estimates:\n- Breakpoint A (left): constrained by (i) depth drop edge, (ii) left_end of spanning pairs: A ∈ [max(left_end)+1, max(left_end)+F-150+1]. With left_ends 19,999,795, 19,999,821 and F≈467±40: A ∈ [19,999,822, 20,000,138].\n- Breakpoint B: right_starts 21,000,014, 21,000,059: B ∈ [20,999,697, 21,000,058].\n- D = span - F: 1,000,563-F, 1,000,492-F; F∈[~427,~507] (median 467, MAD 20 → 95% range ~427–507) → D ≈ 999,985–1,000,136 and 999,985–1,000,065... so D ≈ 1.0000 ± 0.0001 Mb or so.\n\nRounded to the nearest 100 kb: start_100kb = 20,000,000; end_100kb = 21,000,000. size_bp: hmm. Options: 1,000,000 (= rounded end - rounded start), or the best estimate ~1,000,000. Both come out to about 1,000,000.\n\nSo, to pin down the edges, let me do the refinement analysis at 1 kb resolution."}}
