{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"reasoning","text":"Excellent! This is decisive:\n\n1. **The depth drop is razor-sharp**: at 1 kb resolution, depth goes from ~2371 (normal ~2.7x per kb… wait, 2371 per kb = 2.37x, hmm, the local region is a bit low but that's fine) → **exactly 0 at 20,000,000**. The last 1 kb bin with coverage is 19,999,000–20,000,000 (depth 2371), and the first zero bin is 20,000,000–20,001,000. So the left breakpoint A = 20,000,000 is precise (at 1 kb resolution).\n\n2. Right edge: depth is 0 from 20.94 Mb through 20,999,000–21,000,000 (depth 7 — nearly 0), and then jumps to 4311 (2x baseline? 4.3x — hmm, interesting: the first bin after the breakpoint has elevated depth 4311, probably because rightward-protruding fragments pile up at the edge… no wait, that's not right. Actually, on the deleted genome, fragments starting right after breakpoint B: reads start from B onward. Depth at B should be normal… hmm, 4311 vs typical ~2400–3400. The bin right after the edge often shows a small pile-up, because fragments whose left end is just after B cover the first ~150 bp twice (both ends of the fragment)… no. Let me think: at the edge, coverage from fragments entirely to the right of B: normal. But there's also the effect that the fragment length distribution truncates: fragments starting within [B, B+150] contribute left reads covering [B, B+150], and right reads covering [B+F-150, B+F]. Coverage near B from the left reads of such fragments = normal density… actually, a sharp edge in a uniform random fragmentation process causes the coverage to rise linearly from 0… no wait — coverage to the right of B is: fragments cover position p > B if start ∈ [p-F+1, p]. If start positions are uniformly distributed across all of (B, ∞) and also exist to the left of B (fragments starting before B can extend past B only if they span the deletion — those are exactly the breakpoint-spanning pairs with span ~1 Mb; their right reads start around ~B+1… they contribute a tiny amount). Coverage at p = B+k (k small) ≈ density × (number of starts in [B+k-F+1, B+k] ∩ valid). Starts can be at any position > B (leftward is… hmm, in the sample genome, fragments starting before A that span the deletion cover reference [A, B] as a gap — those reads: the left read covers up to A, and the right read starts from B+1. So coverage right after B: from right reads of spanning fragments (sparse, ~2 total) plus reads from fragments starting at ≥ B. For p = B+k: starts ∈ [B+k-F+1, B+k], but starts below B are… wait, starts ∈ (B+k-F+1, B) are only valid if they come from spanning fragments (rare). So coverage at B+k ≈ λ × min(k, F) … rises from 0 over a scale of F≈467 bp, then normal. At 1 kb resolution, the first bin [B, B+1000] ≈ λ × (average over the ramp) ≈ λ×(1000 - ~380) ≈ 0.62×normal×… but we're seeing 4311 > normal (~2400). Hmm, that's elevated, not reduced.\n\nHmm wait, could it be that the right edge of the deletion is not at 21,000,000 but a little before? The bin [20,999,000, 21,000,000] has depth 7 ≈ 0. Bin [21,000,000, 21,001,000] has depth 4311 = 4.3x — high. If B = 21,000,000 exactly, then the ramp-up effect gives the first bin less than normal, not more. Elevated depth at the edge suggests… hmm, reads piling up against the edge? That happens for tandem duplications, not deletions. For deletions, there should be a ramp-down… no wait, ramp-up (from 0 to normal) — the first 1 kb bin averages ~normal×(1 - F/2000) ≈ 0.77×normal ≈ 1850. We see 4311 ≈ 1.5–1.8× normal. Elevated.\n\nHmm! Wait — maybe I mislabeled the coordinates: what if the deletion is actually [20,000,000, 21,000,000), but the simulator placed B somewhere around ~20,999,5xx, and the depth-7 bin [20,999,000, 21,000,000] contains a small tail… and the bin [21,000,000, 21,001,000] contains the ramp-up from B≈20,999,5xx to 21,000,4xx… even so, it shouldn't exceed normal.\n\nAlternative explanation for the elevated first bin: reads with soft-clips/insertions at the edge? I noticed one read with CIGAR 136M10I4M near 21,004,981 — hmm, that's 4.8 kb after the edge.\n\nHmm wait, actually — hold on. What about the spanning-pair right reads? Those start right after B. There are only ~2 such pairs. Not enough to double the depth.\n\nLet me look at the actual data: depth 4311 in the first kb vs ~2100–3400 in subsequent bins. The \"normal\" range there varies a lot (981 to 4922 in adjacent bins!). Looking at the right-side profile: bins vary between 981 and 4922 — high variance (Poisson λ≈2400/1kb, sd≈49; but observed sd is much larger, ~1000 — hmm, actually, the range 981–4922 suggests overdispersion or local GC bias… no wait, these are counts per 1 kb; mean ~2400; but values like 981, 1034, 4922, 4311… sd ≈ 1000+?? That's far beyond Poisson. Hmm, wait — is that right? For 2.7x depth, the count per 1 kb is ~2700 with Poisson sd ~52. The observed fluctuations are much larger… unless the depth profile includes both reads of the pair (counting each base of each read once: total bases = 332k pairs × 300 bp = 99.6 Mb over 40.3 Mb mappable = 2.47x average). Hmm, 2.47x per bp; per kb ≈ 2470 ± 50 (Poisson). But we're seeing swings from 981 to 4922! That's not Poisson. So the coverage is not a uniform Poisson process — there's strong local fluctuation… mappability? GC? Or the simulator sampled non-uniformly? Hmm wait — actually, let me look again at the 100 kb bin numbers: ~270k±15k per 100 kb → sd/mean ≈ 5–6%, much larger than Poisson (1%). So the data is overdispersed relative to Poson (common in real data; maybe the simulator used a fluctuating coverage model).\n\nOK, so the elevated first bin at 21.0 Mb is within the range of overdispersion and/or edge ramp-up. Let me use finer resolution to nail down the right edge precisely: the bin [20,999,000, 21,000,000] has depth 7 ≈ 0 → B ≥ ~20,999,000+; depth is 4311 at 21,000,000 → B ≤ 21,000,000. So B is in (20,999,000, 21,000,000]… hmm, actually, if B were, say, 20,999,300, then [20,999,000, 20,999,300] would be 0 and [20,999,300, 21,000,000] would have a ramp-up up to ~normal×0.5 → bin average ~1200, not 7. Since [20,999,000, 21,000,000] ≈ 0, B must be ≥ 20,999,990 or so (allowing for 7 reads). And depth starts fully at 21,000,000 → B ≤ 21,000,000. So B ≈ 20,999,99x–21,000,000. Combined with spanning-pair right_starts = 21,000,014 and 21,000,059: right reads start from B+1 onward… wait, the right read's reference start = sample_start + D, and sample_start ≥ B+1 (first base after the deletion) → reference start ≥ B + 1 + D - D… hmm, let me redo this: reference_start = sample_start + D, where sample_start ≥ B_sample + 1. Hmm, I need consistent coordinates. Let me define everything in reference coordinates: the sample lacks reference interval [A, B] (inclusive, size D = B-A+1). A sample fragment spanning the deletion: left read aligns to reference end ≤ A-1; right read's reference start ≥ B+1. Observed right_starts: 21,000,014, 21,000,059 ≥ B+1 → B ≤ 21,000,013. And from depth, B ≥ ~20,999,990. So B ∈ [20,999,990, 21,000,013]. Also, the fragment length constraint: right read's sample start = reference_start - D; fragment start = right_sample_start - (F-150) ≥ left_fragment… the left read ends at reference 19,999,795/19,999,821 = sample position (same, upstream of A). Fragment length F = right_sample_end - left_sample_start + 1. Left read sample start = reference left_start = 19,999,646/19,999,672. Right read sample end = reference_right_end - D = 21,000,208 - D / 21,000,163 - D. So F = 21,000,208 - D - 19,999,646 + 1 = 1,000,563 - D; and 1,000,492 - D. F should fall within the insert distribution ~N(467, sd~30-60): D ≈ 1,000,563-467 = 1,000,096 or 1,000,492-467 = 1,000,025. Hmm, the two pairs give D estimates differing by ~71 bp — consistent within the fragment length distribution sd. Best estimate for D: mean span - median F = (1,000,563+1,000,492)/2 - 467 = 1,000,527.5 - 467 = 1,000,060.5 ≈ 1,000,061? Hmm. But the depth edges strongly suggest A = 20,000,000 (exactly, at 1 kb resolution) and B ≈ 21,000,000 (within ~±25 bp). If A=20,000,000 and B=20,999,999 → D = 1,000,000 exactly. The spanning pairs' F estimates: pair 1 F = 1,000,563 - 1,000,000 = 563 (within 467+4sd? sd~20-30 → 563 is ~3-5 sd… hmm, slightly large but possible), pair 2 F = 492 (≈median+1sd). Hmm, pair 1's F=563 is somewhat unlikely, but with insert MAD 20 → tail… let me check the insert distribution tail: p99=500, so 563 is rare (<1%). Hmm. Or D = 1,000,060 (A=20,000,000, B=21,000,059)? Then pair 2's right read starts at B+1 = 21,000,060 > observed 21,000,059. Impossible. Or A=19,999,940?\n\nWell, whatever the exact base, at 100 kb resolution: A→20,000,000, B→21,000,000, size ≈ 1.0 Mb. The exact D is ~1.0000–1.0001 Mb. Precision claim: breakpoints are determined to within ~±1 kb from the depth edge (the 1 kb bins are cleanly on/off), and the spanning pairs support B within ~+320 bp. Honestly: the 1 kb binning is exact (depth goes from 2371 to 0 between bins ending at 20,000,000 and starting at 20,000,000). Could A be somewhere in between… no: the bin [19,999,000, 20,000,000) is fully covered and [20,000,000, 20,001,000) is zero → A ∈ [20,000,000 - a bit, 20,000,000]. Reads in the last covered bin go up to 20,000,000 (the end is ≤20,000,000 since the bin excludes 20,000,000… my profile uses 0-based [a,b) style: cov index (x-a)//w, x is 0-based position; the bin labeled \"20000000\" covers reference positions 20,000,001–20,001,000 in 1-based. Hmm, careful: 1-based reference position p corresponds to 0-based x=p-1. Bin i covers x ∈ [a-1+i*w, a-1+(i+1)*w) → 1-based [a+i*w, a+(i+1)*w - 1]. The transition printed as \"20000000 depth 2371 -> 0\" means bin starting at 1-based 20,000,000 (x from 19,999,999? ugh).\n\nLet me do this carefully: profile(a=19,940,000, b=20,060,000, w=1000). For each read base, 0-based position x ∈ [pos-1, end-1]. cov[(x-a)//w]. Bin i ↔ x ∈ [a+i*w, a+(i+1)*w) ↔ 1-based positions [a+i*w+1, a+(i+1)*w]. Transition printed at i where (a+i*w) = 20,000,000 → i=60: bin 60 ↔ 1-based [20,000,001, 20,001,000] has depth 0. Bin 59 ↔ [19,999,001, 20,000,000] has depth 2371. So the last covered base is ≤ 20,000,000 and the first uncovered base is ≥ 20,000,001. → A (first deleted base) ∈ [20,000,001 - δ...]: actually, A = first deleted reference base: all bases < A are covered; bases ≥ A are uncovered (except for leak). Depth 2371 at 1-based position 20,000,000 → 20,000,000 < A… no wait, covered → not deleted → A ≥ 20,000,001. And 20,000,001 has depth 0 → deleted (or leak) → A ≤ 20,000,001. → A = 20,000,001?? Hmm, or the leak… let me verify the maximum end of reads near the edge — from the reads list: reads in the edge region, e.g., READ_... let me check the maximum end among reads with end ≤ 20,000,00x. The truncated output didn't show the complete edge read list. Let me query precisely: reads with end in [19,999,850, 20,000,150] and start in [19,999,700, 20,000,000]: find the max end. Similarly, the minimum start on the right side: reads with start in [20,999,900, 21,000,200]: find the minimum pos and verify whether any read starts before 21,000,001… and spanning-pair right_reads start at 21,000,014/21,000,059.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. An interesting nuance: if A = 20,000,001, B = 21,000,000, then D = exactly 1,000,000. Then spanning pair 2: F = 1,000,492 - 1,000,000 = 492 ✓ (~median+1.25sd). Pair 1: F = 563 (rare tail ~p99.9?). Hmm. Let me also double-check whether pair 1's right_start=21,000,059 is consistent: right read starts at ≥ B+1 = 21,000,001 ✓. Left_end = 19,999,795 ≤ A-1 = 20,000,000 ✓.\n\nOr A=20,000,000, B=20,999,999, D=1,000,000. In that case, the depth bin [19,999,001, 20,000,000] should lose the last base (20,000,000 is deleted → the read ends by 19,999,999)… the depth would still be ~2371 (losing 1 base is invisible). And the right side: B = 20,999,999 → coverage starts at 21,000,000 ✓ bin [20,999,001, 21,000,000] ≈ 0 ✓ (7 reads… hmm, 7 reads inside the deleted region — leak/mismap).\n\nEither way: D = 1,000,000 ± ~50 bp; rounded coordinates are exactly 20,000,000 and 21,000,000.\n\nLet me get the exact edge evidence: maximum end of reads ending near A from the left, minimum start of reads starting near B from the right, plus the exact positions of the two spanning pairs. Then finalize.\n\nAlso, I should double-check the \"7 reads\" inside [20,999,001, 21,000,000] and the residual reads across 20.0–21.0 (307 reads, mostly MAPQ 0-8, some 23-40 — paralogous mismaps; note the cluster at 20.26–20.32 Mb with mapq 14-40 — this explains the 24,462 bp residual in bin 20.3–20.4: reads from a paralogous locus (segmental duplication) mapping to the deleted region. Those reads' mates — are they consistent? E.g., READ_308867 mapq=40 at 20,279,034 and 20,279,573 (both mates inside the deletion!) — a full pair mapping inside the deleted region with MAPQ 40?? That's odd — unless the pair is small (span ~689, within normal range) and both mates map uniquely within the deleted region — impossible if the sample lacks that region… unless these reads actually come from a paralogous copy elsewhere and that copy's sequence is unique enough to get MAPQ 40 but similar enough to map here. Bowtie2's MAPQ 40 = unique alignment. If a read has a unique best alignment at 20.28 Mb, then the sample has that sequence — but the depth says the sample lacks 20.0–21.0. Contradiction → these must be reads from a nearly identical paralog elsewhere in the genome, and the aligner picked this locus (with a few mismatches). Since the reference is chr22 only, reads from other chromosomes must map somewhere or remain unmapped; only 3 reads remained unmapped — so reads from other chromosomes got mapped to chr22 with mismatches! This is an important QC point: the overall alignment rate of 100% is misleading because the reference is a single chromosome. The residual reads inside the deletion (307 reads ≈ 0.09% of reads) are interchromosomal mismaps/paralogs. OK — that cleanly explains all the residual coverage.\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider whether the deletion is really homozygous. Residuals inside the deletion are ~42 kb of coverage ≈ average 0.04x ≈ 1.6% of the normal 2.5x. If heterozygous, we'd see ~1.25x. Clearly homozygous (or near-homozygous/hemizygous). Let me call it a homozygous/hemizygous deletion.\n\nAlso, let me double-check whether there are any other regions with depth dropping to ~half (heterozygous deletion) — in the normalized 100 kb depth list, everything outside 20–21 Mb was 2.35–2.97. No half-depth regions.\n\nNow — one more sanity check on the other long-span clusters (10.66→16.88, etc.): are these consistent with interchromosomal paralog artifacts? The pattern of pairs with constant span strongly suggests paralogous copies on chr22 itself (segmental duplications). Example: a 6.2189 Mb pair of loci: 10.66 and 16.88 — both pericentromeric/q11 regions, rich in segmental duplications. Not a problem — these are decoys for the analysis; I'll mention them in the report as segmental duplication artifacts and explain why they don't indicate a deletion (normal depth across the entire interval).\n\nWait, hmm, one more consideration: could the real deletion be one of those clusters and the 20–21 Mb zero-depth region be… no. Zero depth = no sample sequence = deletion. Other intervals have normal depth = present in the sample. Deliberation over.\n\nNext, QC metrics for qc.json:\n- read_pairs_total: 332,123\n- read_length: 150\n- bases_total: 99,636,900 (2 × 332,123 × 150)\n- reference: chr22 GRCh38, length 50,818,468, mappable (non-N) length: computed = sum of mapp bins.\n- pairs_both_mapped, pairs_one_mate, unmapped (3 reads).\n- proper_pairs (FLAG 0x2): 169,570 (under -X 500 constraint) — note that this is a tool artifact.\n- insert size: median 467, MAD 20 (from proper FR pairs ≤500), p5/p95.\n- mean depth (mappable): total covered bases / mappable length. Total mapped bases ≈ 664,240 × 150 = 99.6 Mb (minus clipping); mappable chr22 ≈ 38.06 Mb? Let me compute: 50,818,468 - Ns. Ns: 10,510,000 + 2,249,530 + lots of 50k + 100k + 23,171 + 1,131 + 1,500 + 10,000… ≈ 10,510,000+2,249,530+750,000 (15×50k)+100,000+23,171+1,131+1,500+10,000 ≈ 13,645,332. Mappable ≈ 37,173,136. Depth ≈ 99.6/37.17 ≈ 2.68x. ✓ matches the observed ~2.7.\n- depth_inside_deletion (residual): ≈ 0.04x (mismap rate).\n- spanning_pairs supporting the deletion: 2 (with coordinates).\n- soft_clipped_reads: 0 with clip ≥20 bp.\n- bins_half_depth: none (no heterozygous deletion).\n\nDeliverables:\n1. output/deletion.tsv — header: chrom, start_100kb, end_100kb, size_bp, supporting_signals. One row: chr22, 20000000, 21000000, 1000000, supporting_signals string like \"depth_drop=...; discordant_pairs=2; ...\". Keep it parseable: semicolon-separated key=value.\n\nHmm — size_bp: 1,000,000. And I'll note the precise estimate in the report. Good.\n\n2. output/qc.json — comprehensive metrics.\n\n3. output/analysis.py — a single self-contained script that performs the entire analysis (alignment + detection) and writes deletion.tsv, qc.json, and report.md. This is the cleanest interpretation: analysis.py is the script that generated the outputs. Structure:\n   - Step 0 (optional): check bowtie2 tools/ build index — but analysis.py should be runnable to reproduce. Make analysis.py able to (a) build index if missing, (b) align if missing, (c) parse SAM and compute everything, (d) write outputs. Paths relative to workspace root. Robustly with stdlib only.\n\n4. output/report.md — full report: methods, evidence vs. precision, QC summary, results, limitations.\n\nSo next:\n1. Get the exact edge reads (max left end, min right start) and the details of the two spanning pairs (MAPQ, CIGAR).\n2. Compute final numbers (mappable length, total covered bases, etc.).\n\nAfter that, write analysis.py as the canonical pipeline, run it, and verify the outputs.\n"}}
