{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"reasoning","text":"Extremely valuable information:\n\n1. **Left edge**: Maximum read end = 19,999,938 (READ_68545, mapq 42). So coverage extends to at least 19,999,938, and no reads extend past ~19,999,938+ into the deletion region.\n\n2. **Right edge**: READ_250584, pos=20,999,994, mapq=23, CIGAR=4M14I1M7I124M — this is a **split/soft-clipped read at the breakpoint**! It starts at 20,999,994 and has insertions right after 4M (4M14I1M7I124M: 4 bases match, 14 bp insertion, 1 base match, 7 bp insertion, 124 bases match). Hmm, insertions rather than soft clips. Interesting — this read starts 6 bp before 21,000,000, has a messy CIGAR with 14I+7I = 21 bp of inserted sequence, then matches for 124 bp. This looks like a read crossing the breakpoint: the first few bases match upstream of B (positions 20,999,994–20,999,997: 4M), then the read continues with sequence derived from... hmm, insertions mean the read has bases not present in the reference at that location. If the read crosses the deletion junction, then on the sample genome the read continues into sequence that sits at ~B+1... no wait.\n\nHmm wait, let me think about what a junction-crossing read looks like when aligned to the reference. Sample = reference minus [A,B]. A read starting at sample position s with s+149 ≥ ... crossing the junction: the read = reference[s..A-1] + reference[B+1..] (concatenated). When aligned to the reference at position s: the first (A-s) bases match; then the rest of the read is reference sequence starting from B+1, which doesn't match the reference at [A, ...] — the aligner either soft-clips or inserts mismatches. Bowtie2 in local mode would typically soft-clip the non-matching tail. But this read shows 4M14I1M7I124M at pos 20,999,994.\n\nWait — hmm. What if the deletion isn't exact: the simulator may have deleted [20,000,000, 21,000,000) and the junction is... the left read of the spanning pair ends at 19,999,938 — hmm, but earlier I saw left ends at 19,999,795/19,999,821 (the spanning pairs). The maximum ordinary read end is 19,999,938.\n\nAbout READ_250584: pos=20,999,994, 4M14I1M7I124M. Total reference consumption: 4+1+124 = 129 reference bases (20,999,994–21,000,122). Read length: 4+14+1+7+124 = 150 ✓. So the first 4 bases match 20,999,994–20,999,997. Then 14 inserted bases, 1 matching base (20,999,998), 7 inserted, then 124 bases matching 20,999,999–21,000,122. Hmm wait, that's strange: matching reference 20,999,998 and 20,999,999–21,000,122, with 21 inserted bases sandwiched in between...\n\nAlternative interpretation: is this read crossing the left breakpoint A=20,000,000?? No, it's mapped at 20,999,994, near B.\n\nHmm, what if this read is a junction read for B: read = [reference 20,999,994–20,999,997 (4 bp, sample bases immediately before the end of the deletion)] + [sample sequence from...]. Wait — if the deletion is [A,B], the sample has no bases in [A,B], so there's nothing for a read to cross at B from the left — the sample jumps from A-1 directly to B+1. A read crossing the junction starts upstream of A: [ref s..A-1][ref B+1..]. Such a read would map to the left side of A, not near B.\n\nA read mapping near B with insertions means: the read contains 21 bp that don't exist at that location in the reference. Hmm — could this read have originated from the deleted interval's sequence?! If the sample is heterozygous... no wait. If this read came from a homolog that retained the region? But depth inside the interval is ~0, so no.\n\nOr: could the deletion actually be an imprecise event — e.g., the simulator deleted [20,000,001, 20,999,993] and left some bases, or added a small insertion at the junction? Let me look more carefully: the read's 14I+7I inserted bases — if we could get the read sequence and compare it to reference positions near A (20,000,000+), we could tell: if the inserted bases match the reference near 20,000,000–20,000,020, this read is a junction read anchored on the right flank, with its left portion (4+1+7+... hmm).\n\nHmm wait, actually let me think again: maybe this read crosses the junction in the other direction: the read starts from sequence past B (sample) and extends... let me try decoding: sample junction = ref[A-1]|ref[B+1]. A read starting at sample coordinate A-k (k bases upstream of A): read = ref[A-k..A-1] + ref[B+1..B+k']... this read maps partially: its first k bases map to A-k..A-1 (upstream of A, i.e., ~19,999,xxx), not to 20,999,994.\n\nA read starting at sample B+1-t... no, reads starting within t bases downstream of B+1 are entirely to the right of B — no problem, fully matches ref[B+1..] — maps cleanly with pos ≥ B+1 = 21,000,000. READ_250584 maps at 20,999,994 < B+1.\n\nSo how can a read map to 20,999,994 with 4M + insertions + 125M? The read's final 124+1 bases match reference 20,999,998–21,000,122. The leading 4 bp matches 20,999,994–20,999,997. The 21 bp insertion is between them. So this read contains ref[20,999,994..20,999,997] + 21 novel bp + ref[20,999,998..21,000,122]?? That's biologically odd — unless the 21 \"inserted\" bases actually match reference elsewhere — e.g., the tail of the deleted interval! If the deleted interval ended with a sequence similar to...: for example, if the simulator created the deletion with microhomology, or left a small piece behind...\n\nHmm wait wait. Let me reconsider: perhaps the sample deletion isn't exactly [20,000,000, 21,000,000]. What if the sample deletion is [20,000,000, 20,999,972] and there's a small insertion at the junction, or the right edge is 20,999,993 and the 21 inserted bases match ref[20,999,973–20,999,993] (i.e., the simulator's deletion left those bases... no, then they'd be present in the reference and the read would match continuously).\n\nLet me pull the sequence of READ_250584 from the fastq and align it manually against reference substrings around 20,000,000 and 21,000,000. Also check its mate. Its mate: READ_250584/1 or /2 — let me find the mate's position. This is a key read for breakpoint characterization. Also check other reads with indels near the edge (I saw READ_114486 with 136M10I4M at 21,004,981 — that's 5 kb away, probably random noise).\n\nAnother possibility: the 21 bp insertion matches the sequence immediately to the left of A (19,999,97x–19,999,99x). Then the read = ref[20,999,994–20,999,997] + ref[19,999,9xx...] hmm, that would be a strange rearrangement.\n\nOr — maybe I should think this way: the simulator may have generated a deletion with inserted random sequence at the junction, or an \"imprecise\" deletion. Let me compare the 21 bp insertion against the reference near A and near B.\n\nLet me also check the exact CIGAR/sequence of the two spanning pairs (READ_220881: left end 19,999,795, right pos 21,000,059! And READ_256753: left end 19,999,821, right pos 21,000,014). Wait — these are exactly the spanning pairs I found earlier (left_start=19,999,646→READ_220881; left_start=19,999,672→READ_256753). The spanning pair search above output nothing because of the filter condition (a[3]<20_010_000 requires left mate's end ≥20,000,000, but left ends are <20,000,000 — my filter was wrong: `a[1]<20_000_000<a[3]` requires end > 20,000,000). Let me redo it: left mate with pos < 20,000,000 and end in [19,999,600, 20,000,000], and right mate with pos in [21,000,000, 21,000,300]. READ_220881: left (pos=19,999,646, end=19,999,795, + strand, 150M, mapq42), right (pos=21,000,059, end=21,000,208, rev, mapq42). READ_256753: left (19,999,672–19,999,821, +, mapq42), right (21,000,014–21,000,163, rev, mapq42).\n\nWait, both right mates are on the reverse strand, both left mates are on the forward strand: correct FR orientation ✓.\n\nFragment length: READ_220881: sample F = span_ref - D. If D=1,000,000: F = (21,000,208 - 19,999,646 + 1) - 1,000,000 = 1,000,563 - 1,000,000 = 563. READ_256753: F = (21,000,163 - 19,999,672 + 1) - 1,000,000 = 1,000,492 - 1,000,000 = 492. Both are plausible (492 is close