{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! READ_250584/r1's sequence doesn't match what the SAM's CIGAR suggests at that location. Let me verify: the SAM said READ_250584 is at pos=20,999,994, forward strand, 4M14I1M7I124M. But r1's sequence is \"ACCTGTGGATCGGGTTTG...\", and ref[20,999,994-20,999,997] = \"CAGT\" — no match. So the aligned READ_250584 record must be r2 (READ_250584/r2): \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTGAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTG\".\n\nComparing r2's sequence with the reference around 21,000,000:\nref 20,999,985-21,000,130: CAGCTTGGTCAGTCTCAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTGTTCATTCT\n\nr2: CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTGAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTG\n\nFrom \"CGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCG...\" onward, the two match! Let me try aligning manually:\nr2[0:4] = CAGA. Reference near pos 20,999,994? Reference position 20,999,994 (1-based) = the 10th character of the displayed reference segment (starting at 20,999,985): CAGCTTGGTC|AGTCTCAACC → ref[20,999,994..20,999,997] = \"AGTC\"? Hmm, the SAM says 4M matches from 20,999,994. Let me recount: ref segment starts at 20,999,985 = \"CAGCTTGGTC\" (20,999,985-20,999,994), followed by \"AGTCTCAACC\" (20,999,995-21,000,004). So ref[20,999,994] = the \"C\" ending \"CAGCTTGGTC\" (i.e. position 20,999,994 = C), ref[20,999,995-20,999,997] = AGT. So 4M from 20,999,994 = \"CAGT\". r2[0:4] = \"CAGA\". Hmm: C-A-G-A vs C-A-G-T: 3 matches + 1 mismatch?? But bowtie2's CIGAR says 4M... bowtie2's M is sequence match/mismatch (alignment match, can include mismatches; bowtie2 uses M for both). OK, so 4M includes a T→A mismatch at the 4th base.\n\nNext 14I: r2[4:18] = \"ACGAGGACCCAGCA\". Next 1M: r2[18] = \"C\" vs ref[20,999,998] = \"C\" ✓ (ref[20,999,998] = the 5th char of \"AGTCTCAACC\" → A-G-T-C-T-C-A-A-C-C: 20,999,995=A, 996=G, 997=T, 998=C, 999=T, 21,000,000=C, 001=A, 002=A, 003=C, 004=C. So ref[20,999,998]=C ✓.\n\nNext 7I: r2[19:26] = \"TGAGAGG\". Next 124M from ref[20,999,999]: ref[20,999,999..21,000,122] = \"TCAACC\" + \"GCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTGTTCATTCT...\" (need 124 chars: 20,999,999 to 21,000,122).\nr2[26:150] = \"ACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTG\"\n\nHmm, r2[26:150] starts with \"ACCGCAGCC...\" but ref[20,999,999..] starts with \"TCAACGCAGCC...\". Hmm: ref[20,999,999..21,000,004] = T,C,A,A,C,C then \"GCAGCCGCT...\". r2[26:] = \"ACCGCAGCCGCT...\" Hmm, so r2[26:28]=\"AC\" vs ref \"TC\"? Then r2[28:] = \"CGCAGCC...\" hmm wait.\n\nActually wait: 124M from ref[20,999,999]: r2[26:150] should match ref[20,999,999:21,000,123] = T C A A C C G C A G C C G C T G C T C C C G ... Let me write r2[26:]: \"ACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTG\"\nref[20,999,999..]: \"TCAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTGTTCATTCT\"\n\nr2[26:] = \"ACC GCAGCCGCT...\" hmm. ref = \"TCAACC GCAGCCGCT...\". r2's \"ACC\" vs ref's \"TCA\": mismatch at all 3 positions?? Then both continue with \"GCAGCCGCTGCTCCCG...\" Hmm, wait — r2[26:29] = \"ACC\" and ref[20,999,999:21,000,002] = \"TCA\". Then r2[29:] = \"GCAGCC...\" and ref[21,000,002+...] hmm ref[21,000,002] = \"A\"? No that's wrong.\n\nLet me do this computationally rather than by eye. Key hypothesis: READ_250584/r2 is a breakpoint-crossing read: r2 = [sequence matching ref just upstream of A=20,000,000] + [sequence matching ref just downstream of B=21,000,000]. Let me check: ref 19,999,900-20,000,000 = \"CCCTCCCCTGACCTTCTAGAACAAGGGGACAAAGTCACTTTCCCAGCCATCATCTTCCCTGCACTGGAAGGGGCAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTG\".\n\nr2 = \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTGAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGC...\"\n\nLook! r2 starts with \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTG\", which appears at the tail of the upstream reference: ref[19,999,971..20,000,000] = \"...GCAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTG\" — r2[0:28] = \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTG\" matches ref[19,999,973..20,000,000] exactly (28 bp)! And after that, r2[28:] = \"AACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGC...\" vs ref[21,000,000+1..] = hmm ref[21,000,001..] = \"AACC...\" wait ref[20,999,995..21,000,004] = \"AGTCTCAACC\": so ref[21,000,001] = A, [21,000,002] = A? Hmm: 20,999,995=A, 996=G, 997=T, 998=C, 999=T, 21,000,000=C, 21,000,001=A, 21,000,002=A, 21,000,003=C, 21,000,004=C. So ref[21,000,001..] = \"AACC GCAGCCGCTGCTCCCG...\". r2[28:] = \"AACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGCAGGCCTGCAGCTGGCGCTGGGCCTTGTCAAGGGCATCAAGGGCTGCCTCGGCTCGCCGCTTCCAGAGTAAGGCGCTGCCCACCTGGTAGCTGTG\" — yes!! This matches ref from 21,000,001 onward: \"AACC\" ✓ then \"GCAGCCGCT...\" ✓✓✓.\n\nSo READ_250584/r2 = ref[19,999,973..20,000,000] (28 bp) + ref[21,000,001..21,000,122] (122 bp). Total 28+122 = 150 ✓.\n\nThis is a perfect breakpoint-spanning split read!! Breakpoints: last base retained on the left = 20,000,000; first base retained on the right = 21,000,001. So the deletion is exactly [20,000,001, 21,000,000] — size = exactly 1,000,000 bp!! Wait: retained left = ...20,000,000, retained right = 21,000,001... → deleted interval = [20,000,001, 21,000,000], D = 21,000,000 - 20,000,001 + 1 = 1,000,000.\n\nHmm, but the SAM alignment of this read was 4M14I1M7I124M at pos 20,999,994 — bowtie2 aligned it to the right side of B, treating the 5' 28 bp (from the left flank) as 4M (with mismatch) + 14I + 1M + 7I — because the 28 bp left-flank sequence \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTG\" partially matches the region around 20,999,994 (\"CAGT...\" hmm, weak similarity/microhomology). Interesting — but the true structure is a split read.\n\nSo now we have base-resolution breakpoints:\n- A_first_deleted = 20,000,001, B_last_deleted = 21,000,000. Or equivalently: junction = 20,000,000|21,000,001.\n\nHmm wait, but let me double-check the convention. Depth data: the bin [19,999,001, 20,000,000] has depth >0 (position 20,000,000 is covered), and [20,000,001, 20,001,000] has depth = 0. ✓ Consistent: position 20,000,000 is covered (split read READ_250584's 28th base aligns there... and the max end of other reads is 19,999,938). And the right side: position 21,000,001 is the first covered base on the right; the depth bin [20,999,001, 21,000,000] has 7 reads worth of depth — hmm, is the split read covering 21,000,000? Its alignment at 20,999,994 with 4M14I1M7I124M covers reference positions 20,999,994..21,000,122 in the reference span — that's what contributed coverage to [20,999,994, 21,000,000] in my depth calculation (which uses reference span including the I regions — hmm, my coverage calculation counted pos..end, which includes inserted regions — that's why there were 7 reads worth of depth in [20,999,001, 21,000,000]... actually the 7 reads worth might be this read + a few mismapped ones).\n\nHmm, OK wait, but hold on — is the junction really 20,000,000|21,000,001, or could the split read's left part match somewhere else? r2[0:28] matched ref[19,999,973..20,000,000] exactly. Also, the spanning pair left mates end at 19,999,795/19,999,821 — consistent with an upstream flank. Right mates start at 21,000,014/21,000,059 ≥ 21,000,001 ✓.\n\nBut wait, one subtlety: for the deletion [20,000,001, 21,000,000] with D=1,000,000, spanning pair READ_220881's implied fragment length: left mate ref [19,999,646, 19,999,795] (= sample coords), right mate ref [21,000,059, 21,000,208] → sample coords [20,000,059, 20,000,208]... wait no: right mate sample = ref - D = [21,000,059 - 1,000,000, 21,000,208 - 1,000,000] = [20,000,059, 20,000,208]?? That's inside the deleted interval — impossible! Hmm!!\n\nWait, I've confused myself again. Let me redo this very carefully.\n\nSample genome = reference minus the deleted interval [P, Q] (1-based, both inclusive), D = Q - P + 1.\nMapping sample→reference: sample position s. Let A=P. Reference positions < P correspond to sample positions < P (identical). Sample positions ≥ P correspond to reference positions ≥ Q+1: r = s + D.\n\nREAD_220881's left mate maps to reference [19,999,646, 19,999,795] — all < P → sample coords are the same [19,999,646, 19,999,795].\nRight mate maps to reference [21,000,059, 21,000,208] — all > Q → sample coords = ref - D = [20,000,059, 20,000,208].\n\nFor this fragment to be valid in the sample, the sample fragment must be a contiguous interval [19,999,646, 20,000,208] in sample coords. But sample coords ≥ P (=20,000,001) don't exist... wait, sample coords: the sample genome has positions 1..(P-1) corresponding to ref 1..P-1, then sample position P corresponds to ref Q+1 = 21,000,001. So \"sample coord 20,000,059\" — in my formula r = s + D, s = r - D = 21,000,059 - 1,000,000 = 20,000,059 means sample position 20,000,059, which is ≥ P=20,000,001 ✓ — this exists in the sample (it's ref 21,000,059). The sample genome's positions go 1,2,...,20,000,000 (matching ref), then sample pos 20,000,001 = ref 21,000,001, sample pos 20,000,002 = ref 21,000,002, etc. So sample coords [19,999,646, 20,000,208] are contiguous ✓ — spanning the junction at sample pos 20,000,000|20,000,001. Fragment length = 20,000,208 - 19,999,646 + 1 = 563. ✓ Plausible.\n\nPhew, OK. Everything is consistent with deletion = [20,000,001, 21,000,000], D = exactly 1,000,000 bp.\n\nSanity check with READ_250584/r2: read = ref[19,999,973..20,000,000] + ref[21,000,001..21,000,122]. In sample coords: [19,999,973..20,000,000] + [20,000,001..20,000,122] = contiguous sample [19,999,973, 20,000,122], length 150 ✓ perfect.\n\nSo the true deletion: chr22:20,000,001-21,000,000 (1-based, inclusive), exactly 1 Mb. Rounded to 100 kb: start_100kb = 20,000,000, end_100kb = 21,000,000. Size 1,000,000 bp.\n\nHmm, now — about TSV coordinate convention: \"Coordinates are 1-based in reported events\". So we should report in 1-based. Rounding 20,000,001 to the nearest 100 kb gives 20,000,000. Rounding 21,000,000 → 21,000,000. size_bp = 1,000,000.\n\nNote: there's some ambiguity about whether end is the last deleted base (inclusive, 1-based) or a half-open endpoint. Given \"round each breakpoint to the nearest 100 kb\", breakpoints = 20,000,001 → 20,000,000 and 21,000,000 → 21,000,000. size_bp: report 1,000,000 (consistent with both exact and rounded values).\n\nAlso, the report needs to clearly distinguish evidence from precision:\n- Evidence: depth drop (bins), split read with base-level junction 20,000,000|21,000,001, spanning pairs (2), residual coverage statistics.\n- Precision limits: rounded to ±50 kb per the reporting spec; exact junction supported by a single split read and 2 spanning pairs; note that the exact breakpoints are determined by the split read (the strongest single piece of evidence), while the rounded coordinates are robust even if the split read is discounted.\n\nAlso mention: no split reads at the left breakpoint itself (READ_250584 spans it), and expected number of split reads is low (~2x depth).\n\nNow, let me also verify there are no other split reads at the junction (search for reads containing ref[19,999,9xx..20,000,000]+ref[21,000,001..] pattern). Quick check: for each read sequence, check if it contains a 20-mer match adjacent to both breakpoints. Actually, easier: for all reads, check if sequence contains ref[19,999,981..20,000,000] (20 bp ending at A-1=20,000,000) AND ref[21,000,001..21,000,020]. Let me also quickly scan with 15-mer seeds.\n\nAlso, let me double-check the spanning pair mates of READ_250584: its mate r1 = \"ACCTGTGGATCGGGTTTGGG...\" — where does it map? In the SAM, both records for READ_250584: one is 20,999,994 (r2, forward strand), and the mate r1 maps to... the \"reads touching deletion interval\" list contained READ_250584 + pos=20144284 mapq=8 and - pos=20144624 mapq=8 — those are probably READ_232461. Let me check where READ_250584's mate mapped: it might be one of the residual reads inside the deletion (a mismap). If the mate maps uniquely elsewhere, fine. Actually, from the \"inside deletion\" list, I don't see READ_250584 in the first 60 entries... let me verify explicitly.\n\nThen finalize all outputs. Deliverables:\n\n1. `output/deletion.tsv`:\n```\nchrom\tstart_100kb\tend_100kb\tsize_bp\tsupporting_signals\nchr22\t20000000\t21000000\t1000000\tread_depth_drop:1Mb_region_zero_coverage(...);split_reads:1;discordant_spanning_pairs:2;...\n```\nMake supporting_signals compact but informative: e.g., `read_depth_drop(2.68x->0.0x,100kb_bins=10);split_read:1(junction=20000000|21000001);spanning_pairs:2(insert_467bp,spans~1.0Mb)`.\n\n2. `output/qc.json`: structured metrics.\n\n3. `output/analysis.py`: full reproducible pipeline (stdlib-only Python; calls bowtie2 if SAM doesn't exist; otherwise parses; writes all outputs). Make it the script that actually generated the outputs and run it to produce the final files.\n\n4. `output/report.md`.\n\nOne more validation pass to do: scan all read sequences for breakpoint-spanning 20-mers to count split reads (expect ≥1), and check READ_250584's mate position. Then build analysis.py."}}
