de: if read[28:48] == ref[21,000,000:21,000,020] (0-based [20,999,999:21,000,019]) → the first retained base on the right is 21,000,000, meaning position 21,000,000 is not deleted → deletion = [20,000,001, 20,999,999], D = 999,999 bp.\n\nWait, but in my earlier manual check, I had r2[28:] = \"AACCGCAGCC...\" matching ref from 21,000,001 (\"AACC GCAGCC...\"). Let me recompute: ref[20,999,995..21,000,004] = A(995)G(996)T(997)C(998)T(999)C(21,000,000)A(001)A(002)C(003)C(004). Therefore:\n- ref[21,000,000:21,000,020] (0-based slice [20,999,999:21,000,019]) = positions 21,000,000..21,000,019 = C,A,A,C,C,G,C,A,G,C,C,G,C,T,G,C,T,C,C,C... = \"CAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCC\"\n- ref[21,000,001:21,000,021] = \"AACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCG\"\n\nr2[28:48]: r2 = \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTGAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCGCCGCTTGC...\"\nPositions 0-27 = \"CAGAACGAGGACCCAGCACTGAGAGGTG\" (28 characters). r2[28:48] = \"AACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCG\" (counting: A-A-C-C-G-C-A-G-C-C-G-C-T-G-C-T-C-C-C-G = 20 ✓).\n\nTherefore r2[28:48] = \"AACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCCG\" == ref[21,000,001:21,000,021] ✓ NOT ref[21,000,000:21,000,020] (which starts with \"CAACC...\").\n\nBut the scan said jR=0 matched! Why? Because my scan's k-mer set `rightset` included multiple shifted k-mers (j is 0..30), and `has_kmer` only checked membership; then the second loop `for jR in range(0,31)` found the first jR such that kR is contained in s — jR=0 means kR=ref[21,000,000:21,000,020] = \"CAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCC\"... hmm, but this equals r2[27:47]! r2[27] = the last \"G\" of \"...GAGGTG\"? No wait: r2[0:28] ends at index 27 = \"G\" (the G of \"GTG\"). Hmm, r2[27:47] = \"G\" + r2[28:47] = \"GAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCC\" — this is not \"CAACCGCAGCCGCTGCTCCC\".\n\nHmm wait — so how did jR=0 match?? Could it be that... my earlier hypothesis about r2[0:28] is wrong?! Let me re-verify: is r2[0:28] really equal to ref[19,999,973..20,000,000]? The scan said jL=0 and i=8: read[8:28] == ref[19,999,980:20,000,000]. That's a strict statement: read[8:28] = \"AACGAGGACCCAGCAC