{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"reasoning","text":"With this, the answer is now clear:\n\nAll three junction reads split exactly the same way:\n- Left segment = ref ending exactly at 20,000,000 (last retained base = 20,000,000).\n- Right segment starts from exactly 21,000,001 (first retained base = 21,000,001).\n\nREAD_250584/2: ref[19,999,973..20,000,000] + ref[21,000,001..21,000,122]\nREAD_15241/2: 98 bp left + 52 bp right\nREAD_152441/2: 99 bp left + 51 bp right\n\nTherefore deletion = chr22:20,000,001–21,000,000 (1-based inclusive), size = exactly 1,000,000 bp. Junction: 20,000,000|21,000,001.\n\n(The jR=0 report from the earlier scan was a bug in my scan — the second loop found a k-mer match at jR=0, but that was... oh well, the exhaustive split check is the authoritative result: y=21,000,001.)\n\nEvidence summary:\n1. Read depth: ~2.68x outside, ~0x across the entire 100 kb bins from 20.0–21.0 Mb (the only such region on chr22 outside the reference N gaps). At 1 kb resolution, the transition is sharp at 20,000,000|20,000,001 and 21,000,000|21,000,001.\n2. Three soft-clipped/junction reads aligned exactly to the same junction 20,000,000|21,000,001.\n3. Two discordant FR pairs spanning the deletion (READ_220881, READ_256753), with fragment lengths consistent with the library (492 bp, 563 bp vs. median 467).\n4. Residual coverage within the interval is only 0.046x ≈ 1.7% of baseline, consistent with mismapping of reads from other chromosomes (the reference is chr22 only) → homozygous deletion.\n\nRounding (to the nearest 100 kb): start_100kb = 20,000,000; end_100kb = 21,000,000; size = 1,000,000 bp.\n\nNote on the \"end\" convention: I report the end as the last deleted base (inclusive 1-based) = 21,000,000, and state this convention in the report. Rounded breakpoints: 20,000,001→20,000,000; 21,000,000→21,000,000. size_bp = 1,000,000.\n\nNext, build the final deliverables. Plan:\n\n1. Create `output/analysis.py` — a fully self-contained pipeline:\n   - Inputs: ./inputs/find.deletion.r{1,2}.fq.gz, ./inputs/reference/GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz\n   - Steps: decompress to workdir `work/`; build bowtie2 index if needed; align if needed; parse SAM; compute QC; detect deletion (mappability-normalized depth in 100 kb bins; identify zero-depth bins on the largest mappable segment; refine boundaries with 1 kb profile; find junction reads with 20-mer screening; find spanning FR pairs; integrate); write output/deletion.tsv, output/qc.json, output/report.md.\n   - Use the bowtie2 binary in tools/ if available, otherwise try `bowtie2-build`/`bowtie2-align` on PATH.\n   - Make it actually executable and generate the outputs.\n\nDesign decisions for the detection logic in analysis.py (keeping it data-driven, not hardcoded):\n- Build a mappability mask from the reference (non-N runs).\n- Parse SAM: collect per-read records; pair by query name.\n- QC: total pairs, both-mapped pairs, orientation breakdown, insert-size distribution (FR pairs with span ≤ 2000 and mapq≥10... no, better: use FR pairs with span ≤ 5000 for the median; the library median from proper pairs), depth statistics.\n- Depth: count covered bases in 100 kb bins (mapq≥1); normalize by mappable bp per bin; baseline = median of normalized depth over bins with mappability ≥ 50 kb.\n- Candidate deletion: the longest run of consecutive 100 kb bins where normalized depth ≤ 0.1× baseline and mappability ≥ 50 kb (fully deleted). Extend/trim boundaries with a 1 kb profile: find the leftmost kb bin where depth falls below 0.1× baseline, etc. Actually more simply: boundaries from 1 kb profile: find the transition where the 1 kb depth goes from ≥ 0.5× baseline to ≤ 0.1× baseline (left edge), and its mirror image (right edge).\n- Junction reads: for candidate boundaries [A',B'], scan all read sequences: find reads where a prefix exactly matches the reference ending at position p (p ∈ [A'-200, A'+200]) and the suffix matches starting at q (q ∈ [B'-200, B'+200]) (allowing any split t ∈ [15,135]). Implementation: for each read, for each candidate p near the left edge, check whether read[:t] == ref[p-t:p]... that's O(reads × positions) — do it smarter: use the 20-mer membership trick: build the set of 20-mers ending within [A'-200,A'+200] (i.e., ref[p-20:p] for p in the range) and the set of 20-mers starting within [B'-200,B'+200]; a read is a candidate if it contains one of each; then verify exhaustively with split alignment. Efficient enough (664k reads × small sets, using Python's `in` on strings — use `any(kmer in read)` over ~40 kmers... that's 664k × 80 substring searches = 53M — fine, but slow in Python (each `in` is fast C). Probably ~30–60 seconds. Acceptable. Or narrow down: only reads whose mates are... just run it as is; fine.\n- Spanning pairs: FR pairs with both mapq≥10, span > median+5×MAD+1000, left end within [A'-500, A'], right start within [B', B'+500]... no, more simply: span > 10kb and left mate end ≤ A'+200 and right mate start ≥ B'-200, cluster them.\n- Integrate the call: junction = consensus p|q from junction reads (if any); if none, use depth boundaries.\n- Rounding: round(p+1 to nearest 100 kb)... note: breakpoint 1 = first deleted base = p+1 (where p is the last retained base); rounded start = round((p+1)/100000)*100000. Breakpoint 2 = last deleted base = q-1; rounded end = round((q-1)/100000)*100000.\n- size_bp = rounded_end - rounded_start (in TSV, consistent with the reported rounded coordinates); put the exact size in qc.json and the report. Hmm — actually let me think again about what to put in size_bp. The task says: \"Write output/deletion.tsv with chrom,start_100kb,end_100kb,size_bp,supporting_signals. Round breakpoints to the nearest 100 kb and distinguish evidence from precision limits.\"\n\nI think size_bp should be the called deletion size. With exact breakpoints 20,000,001–21,000,000 → size is exactly 1,000,000. Rounded coordinates 20,000,000–21,000,000 also give 1,000,000. No discrepancy here. Set size_bp = 1000000 and document the exact vs. rounded values in supporting_signals/report.\n\n- supporting_signals column: compact key=value list, e.g., `depth_zero_bins=10;depth_ratio_inside=0.017;junction_reads=3;junction=20000000|21000001;spanning_pairs=2;span_sizes=1000563,1000492`. Keep it machine-readable.\n\n2. qc.json contents:\n{\n  \"input\": {...files, read_length, total_pairs, total_bases},\n  \"reference\": {\"name\":\"chr22\",\"assembly\":\"GRCh38\",\"length\":50818468,\"mappable_length\":37071985,\"n_gaps\":[...]},\n  \"alignment\": {\"tool\":\"bowtie2 2.5.5 --very-sensitive\",\"pairs_both_mapped\":332120,\"pairs_one_mate\":3,\"reads_unmapped\":3,\"proper_pairs_flag\":169570,\"note_on_X500\":...},\n  \"library\": {\"orientation\":\"FR\",\"insert_median\":467,\"insert_mad\":20,\"insert_p5\":401,\"insert_p95\":497},\n  \"coverage\": {\"mean_depth_mappable\":2.68,\"baseline_normalized\":2.74,\"depth_inside_deletion\":~0.05,\"depth_ratio_inside\":0.017},\n  \"deletion_call\": {...exact, rounded, evidence counts},\n  \"notes\": [...]\n}\n\n3. report.md: full narrative.\n\nAlso, should analysis.py regenerate the report? Yes — write all three outputs. But report generation within analysis.py makes the script long; that's fine.\n\nAlso, cleanup concerns: intermediate files are in tools/ (decompressed fq, chr22.fa, idx, aln.sam, explore*.py). The task says deliverables go in output/. Leave tools/ as-is (an auxiliary working directory) — hmm, but \"save all requested deliverables to the current workspace\" — tools/ is inside the workspace. Fine. Might be worth moving the canonical alignment into work/ to keep it self-contained... keep analysis.py pointing at the existing tools/aln.sam path (with logic to rebuild if absent). Actually — cleaner: have analysis.py use a `work/` directory (create it, decompress there) and check tools/bowtie2. Hmm, existing artifacts are in tools/; analysis.py can reference them. To keep things tidy and reproducible, analysis.py will:\n- Work dir: `work/` (create). If work/chr22.fa, r1.fq, r2.fq don't exist, decompress them from inputs.\n- Index directory work/idx; alignment work/aln.sam.\n- Bowtie2 binaries: search in tools/bowtie2-2.5.5-mingw-x86_64/, else PATH.\n- Run everything if artifacts are missing.\n\nThen delete the exploratory files in tools/? Not necessary; but move the alignment artifacts into work/ so analysis.py is self-contained. To save time, copy aln.sam into work/ rather than re-aligning (29 seconds). Actually simpler: analysis.py looks for an existing SAM at work/aln.sam; I'll copy the existing one there.\n\nSo, write analysis.py carefully. Key components (all standard library):\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Identify large deletions in low-coverage paired-end hg38 (chr22) data.\"\"\"\nimport gzip, json, os, shutil, statistics, subprocess, sys, collections\n\nWORK = \"work\"\nOUT = \"output\"\nREF_GZ = \"inputs/reference/GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz\"\nR1_GZ = \"inputs/find.deletion.r1.fq.gz\"\nR2_GZ = \"inputs/find.deletion.r2.fq.gz\"\nCHROM = \"chr22\"\nBIN = 100_000\nFINE = 1_000\n\ndef find_bowtie2():\n    candidates = []\n    d = os.path.join(\"tools\")\n    if os.path.isdir(d):\n        for sub in os.listdir(d):\n            for exe in (\"bowtie2-build-s.exe\",\"bowtie2-build-s\"):\n                p = os.path.join(d, sub, exe)\n                if os.path.isfile(p):\n                    candidates.append((os.path.join(d,sub,\"bowtie2-build-s.exe\" if os.name==\"nt\" else \"bowtie2-build-s\"),\n                                       os.path.join(d,sub,\"bowtie2-align-s.exe\" if os.name==\"nt\" else \"bowtie2-align-s\")))\n    for exe in ...: also search PATH\n    ...\n```\n\nThen:\n- prepare(): decompress ref and fq into work/.\n- build_index(): subprocess run.\n- align(): run with --very-sensitive -p N --no-unal.\n- parse_sam(): return list of read tuples + header length.\n- analysis + outputs.\n\nDetection algorithm in the script (generic):\n1. Mappability per BIN