`read.find(kmer)` — that's 1000 × 664k = 664M C-level substring searches... each ~50 ns... ~30–60 seconds. Same order. OK — but note the earlier scan (±30 bp window, 61 kmers) ran in ~15 seconds including fastq I/O. With ±500 bp window (1000 kmers per side), ~15s × (1000/61) × 2 ≈ 8 minutes. Hmm. Optimization: two-stage: first coarse filter with fewer anchor kmers (every 10th position: 100 kmers per side), then validate candidates exhaustively. Or narrow the window with the depth edges (which are within ±1 kb) to ±300 bp. Use ±500 bp with every-5-position subsampling (200 kmers per side), then exhaustive verification. Estimated ~2–3 minutes. Fine.\n\n   Even better: I know exactly what to expect, so the junction screening only needs to run over the candidate region. Keep it generic.\n\n7. Spanning pairs: FR pairs with both mapq ≥ 10, span > max(5×MAD+median+500, 2000), left_end ≤ coarse_left+500, right_start ≥ coarse_right-500, left_end ≥ coarse_left-2000, right_start ≤ coarse_right+2000. Count + list.\n8. Exact junction = mode over junction reads of (p, q).\n9. Rounded coordinates: start_r = round((p+1)/100000)*100000, end_r = round((q-1)/100000)*100000, size_r = end_r - start_r. Note: if the rounding of p+1 falls below... in general, guarantee end_r > start_r.\n10. Write outputs.\n\nAlso compute QC numbers: pairs_total, pairs_both_mapped, reads_unmapped, orientation counts, insert distribution over FR pairs with span ≤ 2000 and mapq≥10 and proper flag, mean depth over mappable positions, depth within the call region, residual ratio, etc.\n\nEdge cases: the 100 kb bin depth uses mapq≥1 — but reads from other chromosomes mismap with low mapq; residual within the deletion is mostly mapq<20. Report both.\n\nReport content (report.md):\n- Task, data, methods (tools and parameters).\n- QC summary table.\n- Evidence:\n  A. Depth (100 kb bins + 1 kb refinement) — with numbers.\n  B. Junction/split reads (3), exact junction 20,000,000|21,000,001, table of read IDs.\n  C. Spanning discordant pairs (2), table, fragment-length consistency.\n  D. Homozygosity assessment (residual 0.046x vs. baseline 2.68x → ratio 1.7% ≈ mismapping background; no heterozygous dip elsewhere).\n- Precision vs. evidence: rounded breakpoints (100 kb) vs. exact junction; note that ±50 kb rounding error is a reporting constraint and the underlying evidence is at base resolution (junction reads) / ~kb resolution (depth) / ~300 bp (spanning pairs).\n- Distractors: segmental-duplication artifacts (long-span pair clusters with normal depth in between), why they are rejected; the half-depth bin at 11–12 Mb is explained by reference N gaps.\n- Limitations: single-chromosome reference → forced mismapping of off-target reads; low depth 2.7x; only 2–3 spanning/junction reads (Poisson limited); bowtie2 -X default.\n\ndeletion.tsv:\n```\nchrom\tstart_100kb\tend_100kb\tsize_bp\tsupporting_signals\nchr22\t20000000\t21000000\t1000000\tread_depth=zero_across_10x100kb_bins(baseline_2.68x,inside_0.05x);junction_reads=3(junction_20000000|21000001);discordant_spanning_pairs=2(spans_1000563bp,1000492bp;insert_median_467bp)\n```\n\nOK, let's write the script. Write it in one go, run it, and check the outputs.\n\nOne more detail: analysis.py needs to record that the aligner was bowtie2 2.5.5 (from tools/). And handle Windows subprocess paths with Chinese characters — we're running with relative paths from the workspace cwd; subprocess should inherit the cwd fine (the earlier run worked).\n\nAlso note: `--threads` for bowtie2-build-s worked. For align, `-p 8`.\n\nInsert distribution: computed from FR pairs with mapq≥10 and span ≤ 5000: median/MAD/p5/p95. Earlier \"proper\" gave median 467, MAD 20 — but with the -X 500 cap, spans 500–5000 among FR pairs could include true longer fragments and discordantly-mapped junk; use span ≤ 2000 for library statistics (expect very few true fragments >500 since p95=497... actually the library seems tight ~467±30; using ≤1000 is safest). Use span ∈