20],[20:40],[40:60],[60:80],[80:100],[100:120],[120:140] lie near L (left) or near R (right) covers all t: a window lies entirely in the prefix if its end ≤ t, entirely in the suffix if its start ≥ t. For any t ∈ [15,135], is there always a window fully on one side? Windows are at [0,20),[20,40),…,[120,140). Prefix [0,t), suffix [t,150). If t ≥ 20, window [0,20) is in prefix. If t < 20, then t ≤ 19 < 20 so window [20,40) is in suffix (start=20 ≥ t ✓) — provided suffix length ≥ 40, i.e., 150-t ≥ 40 ⟺ t ≤ 110 ✓ (t ≤ 19). Good: so checking only the two windows s[0:20] and s[20:40] suffices as a completeness filter?? t≥20 → s[0:20] ⊂ prefix ✓; t<20 → s[20:40] ⊂ suffix ✓. But the filter needs to check left-ness OR right-ness per window: candidate ⟺ (s[0:20] occurs near L) OR (s[20:40] occurs near R)?? No wait: junction reads must match both sides. Filter: (s[:20] found near L) OR (s[20:40] found near R) → for a true junction read: if t≥20 then s[:20] is near L ✓ passes; if t<20 then s[20:40] is near R ✓ passes. False positives will be removed by the exhaustive verification.\n\nBut hmm, note: for the t≥20 case, I'm only checking the left side in the filter; the exhaustive verification will confirm the right side. OK. So the filter is: `seq.find(s[:20], max(0,L-win-20), L+win) != -1 or seq.find(s[20:40], max(0,R-win-1), R+win+19) != -1`. Two find calls per read → fast (~2–4 seconds total). Then exhaustive verification on candidates (few).\n\nAlso, should I use the aligned reads (from SAM) rather than raw fastq? The raw fastq includes all reads (including unmapped). Junction reads might be unmapped or oddly mapped — the raw fastq scan is more robust. Use fastq paths in work/.\n\nNow the spanning-pair logic:\n\n```python\ndef find_spanning_pairs(pairs, left_guess, right_guess, ins_med, ins_hi):\n    out = []\n    for qn, r in pairs.items():\n        if 0 not in r or 0x80 not in r: continue\n        a, b = r[0], r[0x80]\n        if min(a[2], b[2]) < 10: continue\n        left