{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_101","type":"reasoning","text":"Several issues with execution:\n\n1. Rebuilt the index into work/idx (because work/aln.sam existed but idx didn't... no wait, I should have copied aln.sam into work/. But it displayed \"[align] building bowtie2 index\" — because the idx check `idx + \".1.bt2\"` didn't exist. Then it should have skipped alignment (since work/aln.sam existed). OK.\n\n2. **Bug**: `[depth] zero-depth run: bins 200-209 (20000001-21000000); refined edges ~19900001-21100000` — refined edges are wrong: left_guess=19,900,001, right_guess=21,100,000. My refine_edges is broken: it starts scanning from lo = run_bins[0]*BIN - 100_000 = 20,000,000 - 100,000 = 19,900,000. The first fine bin with cov ≤ floor is… hmm, left_guess=19,900,001 means the first fine bin (19,900,001–19,901,000) had cov ≤ threshold?! That's impossible — that region has full depth. Unless there's a bug in the fine coverage calculation. Looking at my refine_edges code:\n\n```python\nfor qn, flag, pos, mq, end, cig in reads:\n    if mq < 1: continue\n    a, b = max(pos - 1, lo), min(end - 1, hi - 1)\n    for x in range(a // FINE, b // FINE + 1):\n        ...\n```\n\nBug: `a // FINE` — a is an absolute 0-based coordinate, but cov's index x is relative to lo! Should be `(a - lo) // FINE`. Same for b. That's the bug: x indexes absolute kb bins, but cov has nb entries relative to lo. For a read at pos ~19,9xx,xxx, a//FINE ≈ 19900+, and nb = (hi-lo)/FINE ≈ 300 → the loop `for x in range(19900, ...)` exceeds nb → range(b//FINE+1) with b//FINE ≈ 19900 → x starts at 19900 > nb → loop body doesn't execute? No wait: range(a//FINE, b//FINE+1) = range(19900, 19901) — x=19900; then `cov[x] += ...` → IndexError?? No error was raised… hmm wait, actually with x=19900 and len(cov)=300 → IndexError. But no error was raised!\n\nHmm wait — actually, the loop body also has a condition: `if lo + x*FINE <= b and ...` — with x=19900: lo + x*FINE = huge value → condition False → cov[x] is never accessed! Phew — so the guard prevented the IndexError, but no coverage was added at all → cov is all zeros → left_guess = lo + 0*FINE + 1 = 19,900,001, right_guess = hi = 21,100,000. That explains \"~19900001-21100000\".\n\nFix: use relative indices: xa = (a - lo) // FINE, etc.\n\n3. `[junction] 0 split reads` and `[pairs] 0 spanning pairs` — consequences of the wrong guesses (left_guess=19,900,001, right_guess=21,100,000 → the windows are off; junction scan ±500 misses; spanning window also misses). Should work with corrected edges.\n\nAlso note that the junction scan ran fast (~15s?), because the filter found nothing (wrong region) → fast path.\n\nAdditionally, the TSV was written with wrong values (start/end derived from p=left_guess-1=19,900,000, q_=21,100,000 → first_deleted=19,900,001, last_deleted=21,099,999 → after rounding 19,900,000 and 21,100,000, size 1,200,000). Need to rerun after the fix.\n\nLet me patch refine_edges in analysis.py. Replacing the loop:\n\n```python\n    for qn, flag, pos, mq, end, cig in reads:\n        if mq < 1:\n            continue\n        a, b = max(pos - 1, lo), min(end - 1, hi - 1)\n        if b < a:\n            continue\n        for x in range((a - lo) // FINE, (b - lo) // FINE + 1):\n            s, e = max(a, lo + x * FINE), min(b, lo + (x + 1) * FINE - 1)\n            cov[x] += e - s + 1\n```\n\nThen the left/right scans remain as-is (they already use relative x with lo + x*FINE).\n\nLet me patch this via a Python-based string replacement.\n"}}
